WEBVTT

00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:02.046
Mia, emergency question.

00:00:02.399 --> 00:00:06.005
You run up the stairs, your heart
races, and you breathe faster.

00:00:06.358 --> 00:00:08.275
What is happening inside your cells?

00:00:09.043 --> 00:00:14.084
They are creating energy, tiny
energy factories, very efficient.

00:00:14.336 --> 00:00:15.298
Five stars.

00:00:15.976 --> 00:00:16.980
Creating energy?

00:00:17.576 --> 00:00:19.904
My imaginary teacher tears are arriving.

00:00:20.155 --> 00:00:22.357
We release energy stored in glucose.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.436
We don't create energy from nothing.

00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:27.092
That wording could cost
you the explanation mark,

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:28.261
depending on the question.

00:00:29.078 --> 00:00:30.590
Release, not create.

00:00:31.014 --> 00:00:31.931
Save the mark.

00:00:32.204 --> 00:00:33.709
Save the teacher's tissues.

00:00:34.509 --> 00:00:35.302
Exactly.

00:00:35.561 --> 00:00:36.831
Mistakes are welcome here.

00:00:36.879 --> 00:00:38.500
We fix them before the exam.

00:00:38.909 --> 00:00:43.375
Today, you will explain how cells release
energy, what happens when oxygen is

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:45.613
limited, and how we investigate it.

00:00:46.374 --> 00:00:49.641
And possibly rescue your tissue budget.

00:00:50.403 --> 00:00:54.824
First question: Is respiration
just a fancy word for breathing?

00:00:55.592 --> 00:00:56.125
Yes.

00:00:56.583 --> 00:00:57.939
Breathing with a lab coat?

00:00:58.762 --> 00:00:59.575
Different jobs.

00:01:00.303 --> 00:01:05.458
Breathing, or ventilation, is the physical
movement of air in and out of your lungs.

00:01:05.913 --> 00:01:08.972
Respiration is a chemical
process in living cells that

00:01:08.973 --> 00:01:10.710
releases energy from glucose.

00:01:11.458 --> 00:01:14.931
So lungs move air, cells do the chemistry.

00:01:15.612 --> 00:01:15.994
Good.

00:01:16.285 --> 00:01:20.449
Watch the delivery route on
our board: lungs, blood, cells.

00:01:20.824 --> 00:01:22.810
Oxygen enters the blood in your lungs.

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:26.691
Blood carries it to cells and
carries carbon dioxide back to

00:01:26.691 --> 00:01:28.199
the lungs to be breathed out.

00:01:28.954 --> 00:01:31.045
Delivery in, rubbish collection out.

00:01:31.432 --> 00:01:32.873
My blood deserves a tip.

00:01:33.634 --> 00:01:36.122
And cells need energy
even while you sleep.

00:01:36.515 --> 00:01:38.576
Your heart and brain
don't close for the night.

00:01:38.925 --> 00:01:42.094
Plants and bacteria respire
too, without needing lungs.

00:01:42.541 --> 00:01:43.963
Here is our glucose card.

00:01:44.379 --> 00:01:46.083
Where does your body get glucose?

00:01:46.654 --> 00:01:49.822
Digesting food, especially carbohydrates.

00:01:50.303 --> 00:01:52.725
So my toast has a secret second career.

00:01:53.469 --> 00:01:54.055
Yes.

00:01:54.608 --> 00:01:57.539
Aerobic respiration
uses glucose and oxygen.

00:01:58.018 --> 00:01:59.898
Aerobic means with oxygen.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.401
Tell me the two reactants,
the substances we start with.

00:02:03.973 --> 00:02:05.490
Glucose and oxygen.

00:02:06.344 --> 00:02:10.254
Now the products appear on the
board: carbon dioxide and water.

00:02:10.841 --> 00:02:12.565
Read the whole word equation.

00:02:13.504 --> 00:02:15.458
Glucose plus oxygen gives

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:19.545
carbon dioxide plus water,
and energy is released.

00:02:20.292 --> 00:02:20.920
Perfect.

00:02:21.442 --> 00:02:25.590
Energy is not another chemical substance,
so we show it as a separate note.

00:02:26.073 --> 00:02:30.085
Some school equations put energy released
in brackets alongside the products.

00:02:30.872 --> 00:02:33.639
No bottle of energy sitting
beside the bottle of water.

00:02:34.117 --> 00:02:34.793
Understood.

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:40.284
Here's a memory hook: glad owls
carry wet eggs, glucose, oxygen,

00:02:40.475 --> 00:02:42.960
carbon dioxide, water, energy.

00:02:43.411 --> 00:02:46.533
But understand the reaction,
don't just memorize the owl.

00:02:47.371 --> 00:02:49.575
I was already designing its raincoat.

00:02:50.322 --> 00:02:51.823
Now a symbol challenge.

00:02:52.386 --> 00:03:00.149
The board shows C6H12O6 plus
6O2 gives 6CO2 plus 6H2O.

00:03:00.646 --> 00:03:03.501
Those big sixes tell us how
many molecules there are.

00:03:04.313 --> 00:03:06.857
Can I change the little
numbers to make it balance?

00:03:07.602 --> 00:03:08.022
No.

00:03:08.433 --> 00:03:11.349
A small subscript is part
of the substance's formula.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.904
Changing it changes the substance.

00:03:14.266 --> 00:03:17.720
We balance by changing the numbers
in front, not the formulae.

00:03:18.371 --> 00:03:20.953
So six water molecules, not

00:03:21.016 --> 00:03:23.140
one very strange water molecule.

00:03:23.893 --> 00:03:26.166
Let's count using our
colored atom counters.

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:29.846
Carbon first, six on the
left, six on the right.

00:03:30.257 --> 00:03:32.829
Hydrogen, 12 on both sides.

00:03:33.132 --> 00:03:34.280
Oxygen is the trap.

00:03:34.698 --> 00:03:35.764
How many on the left?

00:03:36.318 --> 00:03:38.363
12, six pairs of oxygen.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.959
Plus the six oxygen
atoms already in glucose.

00:03:42.216 --> 00:03:43.221
That makes 18.

00:03:43.815 --> 00:03:49.408
On the right, carbon dioxide supplies
12 oxygen atoms, and water supplies six.

00:03:49.844 --> 00:03:50.744
18 again.

00:03:51.501 --> 00:03:54.943
Six carbon, 12 hydrogen, 18 oxygen.

00:03:55.356 --> 00:03:56.451
Nobody vanished.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.010
Exactly.

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:01.164
Atoms rearrange, mass is conserved.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:05.942
Our counters are a simplified model, not
a film of the actual molecular steps.

00:04:06.478 --> 00:04:09.996
Aerobic respiration releases a
relatively large amount of energy

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.516
from each glucose molecule.

00:04:11.882 --> 00:04:13.100
Time for the cell model.

00:04:13.594 --> 00:04:17.354
Which structure is associated with most
of the energy released during aerobic

00:04:17.354 --> 00:04:19.502
respiration in animal and plant cells?

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.205
The nucleus.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:23.440
It looks like the boss's office.

00:04:24.176 --> 00:04:26.591
The nucleus contains genetic information.

00:04:26.991 --> 00:04:28.810
The answer here is mitochondria.

00:04:29.303 --> 00:04:30.891
One is a mitochondrion.

00:04:31.294 --> 00:04:35.108
They are often called the cell's
powerhouses, but remember, energy

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.710
conversion, not energy creation.

00:04:38.479 --> 00:04:41.419
Our cartoon mitochondrion
looks pleased with itself.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:43.235
Does it really have a face?

00:04:44.034 --> 00:04:45.093
Only in this lesson.

00:04:45.954 --> 00:04:49.005
Real mitochondria are
tiny and don't smile.

00:04:49.738 --> 00:04:51.503
Look at the folded inner membrane.

00:04:51.803 --> 00:04:53.314
Why might folds be useful?

00:04:53.983 --> 00:04:56.023
More space without needing a bigger cell?

00:04:56.476 --> 00:04:57.868
Like a folded map?

00:04:58.632 --> 00:05:01.761
Yes, more surface area
for some of the reactions.

00:05:02.192 --> 00:05:03.528
Notice the word most.

00:05:03.963 --> 00:05:06.646
The first stage of glucose
breakdown takes place in the

00:05:06.647 --> 00:05:08.957
cytoplasm, outside mitochondria.

00:05:09.751 --> 00:05:13.123
So not every single step
happens inside the bean.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:14.431
Correct.

00:05:14.926 --> 00:05:18.537
Which would usually need many
mitochondria, a hardworking muscle

00:05:18.541 --> 00:05:20.945
cell or a cell mainly storing fat?

00:05:21.733 --> 00:05:23.807
The muscle cell, because contraction

00:05:23.807 --> 00:05:25.169
needs lots of energy.

00:05:25.913 --> 00:05:26.682
Good reasoning.

00:05:27.159 --> 00:05:30.145
Liver cells also have many
energy-demanding jobs.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:33.626
Sperm cells have mitochondria
in their middle section to help

00:05:33.626 --> 00:05:35.145
supply energy for movement.

00:05:35.541 --> 00:05:37.150
Cell structure fits its job

00:05:38.005 --> 00:05:41.402
More demand, more
energy-releasing equipment, not

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:43.295
a universal bean-counting law

00:05:44.086 --> 00:05:47.254
Exactly, and plant cells
have mitochondria too.

00:05:47.626 --> 00:05:49.356
Don't swap them with chloroplasts.

00:05:49.669 --> 00:05:51.987
Chloroplasts carry out photosynthesis.

00:05:52.616 --> 00:05:56.379
A bonus clue to mitochondria's history
is that they have their own DNA.

00:05:56.695 --> 00:05:59.359
Let's spend some of our
imaginary energy tokens.

00:05:59.611 --> 00:06:03.522
Give me one job that needs
energy Muscle contraction.

00:06:03.895 --> 00:06:07.322
Getting out of bed counts, surely It does.

00:06:08.025 --> 00:06:12.245
Growth and repair also need energy,
including building larger molecules, such

00:06:12.246 --> 00:06:16.850
as proteins, from smaller ones, so does
active transport across cell membranes

00:06:17.693 --> 00:06:21.230
Active transport sounds like taking
the stairs rather than the lift

00:06:22.023 --> 00:06:24.848
Imagine moving particles
from a region with fewer of

00:06:24.848 --> 00:06:26.631
them into a region with more.

00:06:27.008 --> 00:06:29.206
That is against a concentration gradient.

00:06:29.515 --> 00:06:30.748
It requires energy.

00:06:31.301 --> 00:06:34.827
Plant roots can use active
transport to take up mineral ions

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:37.856
So the arrow goes against
the easy direction.

00:06:38.263 --> 00:06:40.046
The tokens pay for the work

00:06:40.742 --> 00:06:41.828
Useful model.

00:06:42.222 --> 00:06:46.426
Energy is also involved in nerve cell
activity and many other processes.

00:06:46.838 --> 00:06:48.478
Respiration releases heat.

00:06:48.768 --> 00:06:52.537
Mammals and birds can use this heat to
help maintain their body temperature

00:06:53.296 --> 00:06:56.624
Then when I sit still, my
cells are still paying bills

00:06:57.325 --> 00:06:58.172
Constantly.

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:00.080
During exercise, muscle
cells need energy faster.
